{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012PNx55DbFMA4hpT2Y1UT6r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, human-readable sizes)\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State:** The project has comprehensive specification documents across multiple releases, but no implementation code yet. The first release is focused on `ts` (timestamp stdin lines), with its use case currently in progress.\n\n**Roadmap:** Starting with rel01.0 and the ts utility.\n\n**Priority Analysis:** To complete rel01.0, I need to build the differential testing harness in `pkg/testutils/` and the ts implementation in `cmd/ts/`. The ts utility doesn't depend on pkg/sys or pkg/format, so I can focus on just these two components.\n\n**Dependency Order:** pkg/testutils is the foundation that everything else depends on, so I should build that first, then move to cmd/ts which relies on it for differential testing.\n\n**Task Sizing:** Since I'm limited to one task, implementing pkg/testutils makes the most sense as the critical blocker. The core harness includes the DiffTest struct, the RunDiffTests function for executing binaries with timeouts, comparison and reporting logic, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison capabilities—all of which form a cohesive, scoped piece of work.\n\n**Line Count Estimate:** Breaking down the components—the struct definitions and types, the main test runner function, binary execution logic, comparison and reporting, normalization with the TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison—comes to roughly 290 lines of production code, which fits the target range.\n\n**Constraint Check:** I need exactly 1 task, so I'm focusing on the pkg/testutils implementation as the foundation. Since the project specs are written but implementation is pending, I'll assume the package doesn't exist yet and craft a self-contained task description for the stitch agent.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74308,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74308},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb7bc6d6-2b9b-4f62-9b65-88150f3139d8","uuid":"55aeaac4-90f0-4c84-ae89-a145c0b595ea"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012PNx55DbFMA4hpT2Y1UT6r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the project context, here's my analysis:\n\n**Project state:** go-unix-utils is a spec-driven pipeline that regenerates Unix utilities in Go, verified by differential testing against GNU reference binaries. All specification artifacts (PRDs, use cases, test suites) exist for releases rel01.0 through rel02.1, but no implementation code has been written yet.\n\n**Current release:** rel01.0 (in_progress) -- the single use case is `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (timestamp stdin lines via ts).\n\n**Priority reasoning:** To implement cmd/ts, the differential testing harness `pkg/testutils` must exist first. It is a dependency of every utility in the project. The dependency chain is: pkg/testutils -> cmd/ts. Since I'm limited to 1 task, the highest-value task is implementing pkg/testutils per prd001-testutils. It targets roughly 250-300 lines of production code across 2-3 files, which fits the sizing constraint."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74308,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74308},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb7bc6d6-2b9b-4f62-9b65-88150f3139d8","uuid":"194d0290-bf35-474c-b198-0f0263e99898"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012PNx55DbFMA4hpT2Y1UT6r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E8BXwop1fpgdBRG6jwNs1e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/petardjukic/WORKSPACE/go-unix-utils/.cobbler/measure-20260223-221955.yaml","content":"- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml (full PRD for pkg/testutils -- defines DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests API, normalization hooks, file-state comparison, and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component description and its role as the acceptance gate for the pipeline)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.0.yaml (test suite for ts -- shows how TimestampNormalizer and RunDiffTests are consumed by cmd/ts tests; confirms the API shape the harness must expose)\n      - go.mod (module path and Go version)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/testutils.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"DiffTest struct, Normalize function type, RunDiffTests entry point, binary execution, comparison and reporting, file-state comparison\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalizers.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"TimestampNormalizer and ComposeNormalizers helper\"\n      - path: pkg/testutils/testutils_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Unit tests for RunDiffTests, normalization composition, TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Define a DiffTest struct in pkg/testutils/testutils.go with these fields:\n            - Name string (used as the t.Run subtest name)\n            - Args []string (command-line arguments passed to both binaries)\n            - Stdin []byte (stdin content; nil means no stdin / immediate EOF)\n            - Env []string (environment variable overrides; nil means inherit test process env)\n            - ExitCode int (expected exit code; compared against both binaries)\n            - Normalize []func([]byte) []byte (slice of normalization functions applied in order before comparison; nil or empty means no normalization)\n            - WorkDir string (optional working directory for both binaries; empty means use a per-test temp dir created by the harness)\n            - ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte (optional; maps relative file paths to expected content after execution, for utilities like sponge whose output is a file rather than stdout)\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement a RunDiffTests function with signature:\n            func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest)\n          For each DiffTest in the slice, call t.Run(test.Name, ...) to create a named subtest. Inside\n          the subtest, execute both goBinary and refBinary with identical Args, Stdin, Env, and WorkDir.\n          Use os/exec.Command. Set cmd.Stdin from test.Stdin (use bytes.NewReader; if nil, set to nil\n          for immediate EOF). Set cmd.Env by merging os.Environ() with test.Env overrides (later entries\n          override earlier ones for the same key). Capture stdout and stderr into bytes.Buffer. Capture\n          the exit code from the *exec.ExitError or 0 on success.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. Use context.WithTimeout with a\n          default of 10 seconds. Accept an optional Timeout time.Duration field on DiffTest (zero\n          means use default). If either binary exceeds the timeout, fail the subtest with a message\n          that includes which binary timed out, the args, and the timeout duration.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          After both binaries complete, apply the normalization chain. If test.Normalize is non-nil\n          and non-empty, apply each function in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr bytes.\n          The functions are applied sequentially: output of function N is input to function N+1.\n      - id: R5\n        text: |\n          Compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte. Compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte. Compare\n          exit codes exactly. If any differ, call t.Errorf with a failure message that includes:\n          the DiffTest.Args, the Stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer with a note\n          \"(truncated)\"), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference stderr,\n          the Go stderr, both exit codes. A passing test must produce no output.\n      - id: R6\n        text: |\n          When test.ExpectedFiles is non-nil, after both binaries complete, for each entry in\n          ExpectedFiles read the file at the relative path (relative to the binary's WorkDir) and\n          compare its content byte-for-byte to the expected []byte value. Report divergence in the\n          same format as stdout divergence (showing expected vs actual file content).\n      - id: R7\n        text: |\n          When test.WorkDir is empty, create a temporary directory via t.TempDir() for each binary\n          invocation. The Go binary and reference binary each get their own temp dir to avoid\n          interference. For file-state comparison (ExpectedFiles), run both binaries in separate\n          copies of the same initial directory state -- copy the initial WorkDir contents to both\n          temp dirs before execution, then compare the expected files in each temp dir.\n      - id: R8\n        text: |\n          Implement TimestampNormalizer in pkg/testutils/normalizers.go as a func([]byte) []byte\n          that replaces common timestamp patterns with the fixed string \"TIMESTAMP\". Patterns to\n          match (using regexp):\n            - Syslog format: \"Jan  5 14:30:00\" or \"Feb 19 12:34:56\" -- abbreviated month, space-padded day, HH:MM:SS\n            - ISO-8601: \"2024-01-05T14:30:00\" with optional fractional seconds and Z or timezone offset\n            - HH:MM:SS standalone (for -s and -i elapsed mode output)\n            - Epoch with microseconds: digits.digits pattern like \"1708358732.001234\"\n          The normalizer must handle multiple timestamps on the same line and across multiple lines.\n      - id: R9\n        text: |\n          Implement a ComposeNormalizers function with signature:\n            func ComposeNormalizers(fns ...func([]byte) []byte) func([]byte) []byte\n          that returns a single normalizer applying all functions in order. This is a convenience\n          for building the Normalize slice from individual normalizers.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Package name is \"testutils\" under module path github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils.\n          The package is imported by cmd/<utility>/ test files. It must not import any cmd/ package.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Use os/exec for binary execution. Do not shell out via bash or sh. Pass args directly\n          to exec.Command(binary, args...). This avoids shell quoting issues and matches how\n          Go test binaries are invoked.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          For environment merging, iterate os.Environ() first, then append test.Env entries.\n          If test.Env contains a key already in os.Environ(), the test.Env value wins because\n          exec.Cmd.Env uses the last occurrence of a key.\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          The only external dependency allowed is golang.org/x/sys (already in go.mod per the\n          architecture). pkg/testutils itself should need only the Go standard library.\n      - id: D5\n        text: |\n          For the test file (testutils_test.go), write tests that use a simple test binary.\n          Create a small Go program in testdata/ (e.g., testdata/echo/main.go) that echoes\n          stdin to stdout with optional prefix, so RunDiffTests can be tested without requiring\n          any Homebrew reference binary. Build it with \"go build\" in TestMain or use \"go run\"\n          with the testdata path. This makes the tests self-contained and runnable in CI without\n          brew dependencies.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: |\n          pkg/testutils compiles with \"go build ./pkg/testutils/...\" and produces no errors.\n      - id: AC2\n        text: |\n          RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest visible in \"go test -v\" output.\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          A test case where both binaries produce identical output passes silently (no t.Errorf).\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          A test case where stdout differs between binaries fails with a message showing args,\n          stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes.\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          A test case that exceeds the timeout fails with a clear timeout message rather than\n          hanging the test run.\n      - id: AC6\n        text: |\n          TimestampNormalizer replaces syslog-format timestamps (\"Feb 19 12:34:56\") with\n          \"TIMESTAMP\" and replaces HH:MM:SS patterns with \"TIMESTAMP\".\n      - id: AC7\n        text: |\n          ComposeNormalizers applies normalizers in order -- output of first is input to second.\n      - id: AC8\n        text: |\n          File-state comparison (ExpectedFiles) correctly detects when a binary writes different\n          file content than expected.\n      - id: AC9\n        text: |\n          \"go test ./pkg/testutils/...\" passes with all tests green.\n      - id: AC10\n        text: |\n          golangci-lint run ./pkg/testutils/... produces no warnings. If golangci-lint is not\n          installed or not configured, skip this check but ensure \"go vet ./pkg/testutils/...\"\n          produces no warnings.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":74308,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":74308},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb7bc6d6-2b9b-4f62-9b65-88150f3139d8","uuid":"6dfa609b-1433-412a-aed2-3b3c7a1fa7ec"}
